{"reference":"Judges 9","book":"Judges","chapter":9,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Judges in Context","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Judges as a whole moves from incomplete possession and covenant indictment through repeated deliverance stories toward internal idolatry, atrocity, and civil war. Abimelech’s violent rise and the destruction that follows belong within that larger movement: the conflict is no longer simply with an outside oppressor, but takes place within Israel itself.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_judges_009_9b725809739f5e23042db895"]},{"id":"block_2","text":"One reading sees Gideon’s story and Abimelech’s rule as a turning point in which deliverance becomes entangled with dynastic ambition, idolatry, and violence within Israel. The book’s repeated concern about the absence of a king raises a question about kingship, but Abimelech’s rule does not itself endorse every form of monarchy.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_judges_009_f6e5c734fe3240c079385ce3"]}]}],"generated_metadata":null}